    <!-- Onboarding and disclaimers -->
    <string name="onboarding_title">Track your pet\'s breathing</string>
    <string name="onboarding_subtitle">A simple tool to measure and record your dog\'s or cat\'s resting respiratory rate, especially helpful for pets with heart conditions.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_point_measure">Tap once per breath for 30 or 60 seconds, and PetBreath works out breaths per minute.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_point_private">No account and no internet. Everything stays on this phone.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_point_vet">Share a PDF or CSV report with your veterinarian.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_accept">I understand, continue</string>
    <string name="disclaimer_short">PetBreath does not diagnose medical conditions. Always follow your veterinarian\'s advice. In an emergency, contact a vet immediately.</string>
    <string name="disclaimer_full">PetBreath is a record-keeping tool. It does not diagnose, treat or prevent any disease and is not a substitute for professional veterinary care. Readings are counted by you and may be inaccurate. The ranges in the app are general references or values you entered; your veterinarian can tell you what is normal for your pet. If you are worried about your pet\'s breathing, contact your veterinarian. If your pet is struggling to breathe, seek emergency care right away.</string>

    <!-- Education -->
    <string name="learn_title">About resting breathing</string>
    <string name="learn_what_title">What is the resting respiratory rate?</string>
    <string name="learn_what_body">The resting respiratory rate (RRR), also called the sleeping respiratory rate, is the number of breaths your pet takes in one minute while asleep or completely relaxed. One breath is a full rise and fall of the chest (breathing in and out).</string>
    <string name="learn_why_title">Why measure it?</string>
    <string name="learn_why_body">For dogs and cats with heart disease, a rising resting breathing rate can be one of the earliest signs that fluid is building up in or around the lungs, often before other symptoms appear. Many veterinarians ask owners of pets with heart conditions to check it regularly at home and to keep a record. Tracking it over time also helps your vet see how your pet responds to treatment.</string>
    <string name="learn_how_title">How to measure</string>
    <string name="learn_how_body">• Measure when your pet is deeply asleep or fully at rest in a cool, calm place, not right after exercise, eating, excitement or in hot weather.\n• Don\'t count while your pet is dreaming, purring, panting or sniffing.\n• Watch the chest or belly. Tap the screen once each time the chest rises.\n• Use the 30-second mode for a quick reading or 60 seconds for more accuracy. PetBreath converts the count to breaths per minute.\n• Measuring at a similar time each day makes trends easier to compare.</string>
    <string name="learn_normal_title">What is normal?</string>
    <string name="learn_normal_body">Most healthy dogs and cats have a sleeping respiratory rate below 30 breaths per minute, and many breathe considerably slower. A rate above 30 breaths per minute is widely used as a reference threshold, which is why PetBreath uses 30 as its default. Your veterinarian may set a different range for your pet. You can change it in the pet\'s profile. Your pet\'s own usual rate (their baseline) is just as important as any single number.</string>
    <string name="learn_contact_title">When to contact your veterinarian</string>
    <string name="learn_contact_body">Call your veterinarian if:\n• the resting rate is above your pet\'s threshold on more than one measurement, especially if taken a little while apart,\n• the rate is clearly higher than your pet\'s usual baseline, or keeps rising over several days,\n• your pet also coughs, tires easily, is restless at night, eats less or seems unwell,\n• your vet has given you specific instructions and a reading meets them.</string>
    <string name="learn_emergency_title">Get emergency care immediately if</string>
    <string name="learn_emergency_body">• breathing is labored, noisy or uses a lot of belly effort,\n• your pet breathes with an open mouth (especially a cat) or cannot settle down or lie comfortably,\n• gums or tongue look pale, gray, blue or purple,\n• your pet collapses, faints or is extremely weak.\nDo not wait to record another measurement in these situations.</string>
    <string name="learn_disclaimer_title">Important</string>
